\section{Details}{

\subsection{Constructors}{

\code{new(GDALMultiDimRaster, filename)}\cr
Constructor for read-only access. \code{filename} is a character string
containing the file name or other data source name (DSN) of a
multidimensional raster, e.g., a netCDF file, a Zarr store given as
\code{ZARR:"/vsicurl/https://..."}, or a multidimensional VRT. Returns an
object of class \code{GDALMultiDimRaster}, or an error is raised if the dataset
cannot be opened in multidimensional mode.

\code{new(GDALMultiDimRaster, filename, read_only)}\cr
Constructor specifying read/write access (\code{read_only = TRUE|FALSE}).

\code{new(GDALMultiDimRaster, filename, read_only, open_options)}\cr
Constructor specifying dataset \code{open_options} as a character vector of
\code{NAME=VALUE} pairs.

\code{new(GDALMultiDimRaster, filename, read_only, open_options, shared, allowed_drivers)}\cr
Constructor to open the dataset in shared mode (\code{shared = TRUE}), and/or to
give \code{allowed_drivers} as a character vector of driver short names that
must be considered when opening \code{filename}. All arguments are required in
this form of the constructor, but \code{open_options} and \code{allowed_drivers} may
be \code{NULL}.
}

\subsection{Read/write fields}{

\code{$infoOptions}\cr
A character vector of command-line arguments for \command{gdalmdiminfo}
used by the \code{$info()} method (see below), e.g.,
\code{c("-detailed", "-limit", "5")}. Defaults to an empty vector (no options).

\code{$quiet}\cr
A logical value, \code{TRUE} to suppress progress reporting and other console
output from class methods. Defaults to \code{FALSE}. Reserved for methods
added in later releases.
}

\subsection{Methods}{

\code{$getFilename()}\cr
Returns a character string containing the \code{filename} associated with this
\code{GDALMultiDimRaster} object.

\code{$open(read_only)}\cr
(Re-)opens the dataset on the existing filename. Use this method to
open a dataset that has been closed using \code{$close()}. May be used to
re-open a dataset with a different read/write access (\code{read_only} set to
\code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}). The method will first close an open dataset, so it is
not required to call \code{$close()} explicitly in this case.
No return value, called for side effects.

\code{$isOpen()}\cr
Returns logical \code{TRUE} if the dataset is open, otherwise \code{FALSE}.

\code{$isReadOnly()}\cr
Returns logical \code{TRUE} if the dataset is open read-only, or \code{FALSE} if
opened for update.

\code{$getDriverShortName()}\cr
Returns the short name of the GDAL driver used to open the dataset
(e.g., \code{"netCDF"}, \code{"Zarr"}).

\code{$getDriverLongName()}\cr
Returns the long name of the GDAL driver used to open the dataset.

\code{$getFileList()}\cr
Returns a character vector of files believed to be part of the dataset.
If it returns an empty string (\code{""}) it means there is believed to be no
local file system files associated with the dataset (e.g., a virtual file
system).

\code{$info()}\cr
Prints various information about the dataset to the console, as JSON
output from the \command{gdalmdiminfo} utility, using the options given in
\code{$infoOptions}. No return value, called for that side effect. See also
\code{\link[=mdim_info]{mdim_info()}}, which returns the information as a character string.

\code{$getGroupNames(path)}\cr
Returns a character vector containing the names of the sub-groups directly
under the group at \code{path} (a character string giving the full path of a
group, e.g., \code{"/"} for the root group). Returns an empty vector if there
are no sub-groups. An error is raised if no group is found at \code{path}.

\code{$getMDArrayNames(path)}\cr
Returns a character vector containing the names of the arrays in the group
at \code{path}. Coordinate variables (indexing variables of dimensions) are
included. An error is raised if no group is found at \code{path}.

\code{$getDimensions(path)}\cr
Returns a data frame describing the dimensions of the group or the array at
\code{path}. For an array, rows are in the array's own dimension order (slowest
varying first). For a group, rows are the dimensions defined in that group,
in the order returned by the driver. The data frame has columns:
\itemize{
\item \code{name}: the dimension name
\item \code{full_name}: the full path of the dimension
\item \code{size}: the dimension size (numeric)
\item \code{type}: the dimension type, e.g., \code{"HORIZONTAL_X"}, \code{"HORIZONTAL_Y"},
\code{"VERTICAL"}, \code{"TEMPORAL"}, or \code{""} if unknown
\item \code{direction}: the dimension direction, e.g., \code{"EAST"}, \code{"NORTH"},
\code{"FUTURE"}, or \code{""} if unknown
\item \code{indexing_variable}: the full path of the array holding the coordinate
values of the dimension, or \code{""} if there is none.
}

A \code{path} is first looked up as a group, then as an array. An error is
raised if neither is found.

\code{$getAttributes(path)}\cr
Returns a named list of the attributes of the group or the array at \code{path}.
Values are converted by attribute data type: string attributes are returned
as \code{character}, integer attributes that fit in 32 bits as \code{integer}, other
numeric attributes as \code{double}, and attributes of compound or complex data
type as \code{raw} (the bytes as stored). Multi-valued attributes are returned
as vectors. Returns an empty list if there are no attributes. Note that
some drivers report certain attributes through dedicated properties of an
array instead (e.g., the netCDF driver hides \code{units}, \verb{_FillValue},
\code{scale_factor} and \code{add_offset}, which will be available from methods of
class \code{GDALMDArray}). A \code{path} is first looked up as a group, then as an
array. An error is raised if neither is found.

\code{$getGroupInfo(path)}\cr
Returns a named list describing the group at \code{path}, with elements
\code{name}, \code{full_name}, \code{group_names}, \code{array_names}, \code{dimensions} (a data
frame as returned by \code{$getDimensions()}), and \code{attributes} (a list as
returned by \code{$getAttributes()}). An error is raised if no group is
found at \code{path}.

\code{$openMDArray(path)}\cr
Opens the array at \code{path} (a character string giving the full path of an
array, e.g., \code{"/temperature"} or \code{"/group/temperature"}) and returns an
object of class \code{\link{GDALMDArray}}. The array holds a reference to
the dataset, so it remains valid after \code{$close()} is called on this
object. An error is raised if no array is found at \code{path}.
}

\subsection{Creating groups, dimensions, arrays and attributes}{

The methods below require a dataset open for update, i.e., opened with
\code{read_only = FALSE} or created with \code{\link[=mdim_create]{mdim_create()}}, and a format driver
that supports the operation. An error is raised if the operation fails.
They have no return value unless stated otherwise. Changes may not be
written to the file until the dataset is closed, which happens when
\code{$close()} has been called on this object and on every array object
obtained from it. That includes arrays that were not assigned a name, as
in \code{ds$openMDArray("/x")$read(NULL, NULL, NULL)}, which stay open until
garbage collected (see \code{\link[=gc]{gc()}}). For the same reason, a file may fail to
re-open for update while array objects from an earlier open still exist.

\code{$createGroup(path, options)}\cr
Creates a group. \code{path} is the full path of the new group, e.g.,
\code{"/ocean"} or \code{"/ocean/surface"}. Its parent group must exist. \code{options}
is a character vector of driver-specific \code{NAME=VALUE} group creation
options, or \code{NULL}.

\code{$createDimension(path, name, type, direction, size, options)}\cr
Creates a dimension in the group at \code{path} (\code{"/"} for the root group).
\code{name} is the name of the dimension, so that its full path is, e.g.,
\code{"/time"} for \code{path = "/"} and \code{name = "time"}. \code{type} is a character
string, generally one of \code{"HORIZONTAL_X"}, \code{"HORIZONTAL_Y"}, \code{"VERTICAL"},
\code{"TEMPORAL"}, \code{"PARAMETRIC"} or \code{""} (no type). \code{direction} is a character
string, generally one of \code{"EAST"}, \code{"WEST"}, \code{"SOUTH"}, \code{"NORTH"}, \code{"UP"},
\code{"DOWN"}, \code{"FUTURE"}, \code{"PAST"} or \code{""}. \code{size} is the number of values
along the dimension. How \code{type} and \code{direction} are stored depends on the
format, and they may read back differently after the dataset is re-opened
(e.g., netCDF does not store them, and derives them from CF attributes of
the indexing variable such as its unit). \code{options} is a character vector
of driver-specific \code{NAME=VALUE} dimension creation options, or \code{NULL},
e.g., \code{"UNLIMITED=YES"} for netCDF.

\code{$createMDArray(path, dim_paths, data_type, options)}\cr
Creates an array and returns it as an object of class
\code{\link{GDALMDArray}}. \code{path} is the
full path of the new array, e.g., \code{"/temperature"}. Its parent group must
exist. \code{dim_paths} is a character vector of the full paths of its
dimensions, in order, e.g., \code{c("/time", "/lat", "/lon")} (see
\code{$createDimension()}), or \code{NULL} (or \code{character(0)}) for an array
with no dimensions (a single value). \code{data_type} is a character string
giving a GDAL data type name (e.g., \code{"Byte"}, \code{"Int16"}, \code{"Int32"},
\code{"Float32"}, \code{"Float64"}, see \code{\link{DEFAULT_NODATA}} for the full list), or
\code{"String"} for an array of variable-length strings (not supported by all
formats).
\code{options} is a character vector of driver-specific \code{NAME=VALUE} array
creation options, or \code{NULL}, e.g., \code{"BLOCKSIZE=1,180,360"} and
\code{"COMPRESS=DEFLATE"} for netCDF (see the documentation of the format
driver). A 1D array with the same full path as its dimension (the same
group and the same name, e.g., array \code{"/time"} on dimension \code{"/time"}) is
set as the indexing variable of that dimension, i.e., it holds the
coordinate values of the dimension.

\code{$setAttribute(path, name, value, data_type)}\cr
Sets the attribute \code{name} of the group or array at \code{path}. \code{value} is a
character, numeric, integer or logical vector, of length \code{1} or more and
with no \code{NA} values (\code{NaN} is accepted in a numeric vector). \code{data_type}
is the data type of the attribute. With \code{""} it follows \code{value}: String
for character, Float64 for numeric, and Int32 for integer or logical.
Otherwise give a GDAL data type name for numeric, integer or logical
values (e.g., \code{"Int16"}, \code{"Float32"}), which are converted to that type,
or \code{"String"} for character values. This matters for attributes that, by
convention, have the data type of their array (e.g., \code{valid_range},
\code{valid_min} and \code{valid_max} in the CF conventions). Factors and
\code{integer64} vectors are not accepted, convert them first. An attribute
that already exists is replaced by deleting it and creating it again, so
that its type and length may change (requires GDAL >= 3.8, otherwise the
result depends on the format driver). The replacement is not atomic: if
the new value cannot be written, the previous attribute is not restored.
Some formats restrict certain attributes, e.g., netCDF requires
\verb{_FillValue} to have the data type of its array.

\code{$deleteAttribute(path, name)}\cr
Deletes the attribute \code{name} of the group or array at \code{path}. Requires
GDAL >= 3.8.

\code{$close()}\cr
Closes the dataset and the root group. No return value, called for side
effects.
}
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (gdal_version_num() >= gdal_compute_version(3, 2, 0) && isTRUE(gdal_formats("netCDF")$multidim_raster)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
f <- system.file("extdata/mdim_small.nc", package="gdalraster")
ds <- new(GDALMultiDimRaster, f)
ds

ds$getGroupNames("/")
ds$getMDArrayNames("/")
ds$getMDArrayNames("/ocean")

ds$getDimensions("/")
ds$getDimensions("/t2m")

ds$getAttributes("/")
ds$getAttributes("/time")

str(ds$getGroupInfo("/ocean"))

arr <- ds$openMDArray("/ocean/sst")
arr$getDimensions()
arr$close()

ds$infoOptions <- c("-array", "t2m", "-detailed", "-limit", "2")
ds$info()

ds$close()

## create a new dataset, see also mdim_create()
f_new <- tempfile(fileext = ".nc")
ds <- mdim_create("netCDF", f_new)
ds$createDimension("/", "x", "HORIZONTAL_X", "EAST", 3, NULL)
x <- ds$createMDArray("/x", "/x", "Float64", NULL)
ds$setAttribute("/", "title", "a new dataset", "")
ds$setAttribute("/x", "units", "degrees_east", "")
x$close()
ds$close()

ds <- new(GDALMultiDimRaster, f_new)
ds$getAttributes("/")$title
ds$getDimensions("/")
ds$close()
\dontshow{unlink(f_new)}
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
